      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build a small owner with a saved refresh callback. Keep empty success and failure separate, then safely call the callback after releasing its owner.",
        "steps": [
          "Before reading the answer, declare an outcome enum, a throwing service protocol, and a fake with a known result.",
          "Write a final class with state and an optional stored refresh callback. Choose and explain its capture strength.",
          "Write a switch covering every outcome. Give initial state, no matches, names, and retry different summaries.",
          "Save the callback separately. Release its owner, check a weak probe, and call the saved callback safely.",
          "Show offline failure separately. Predict the printed output before you run the program.",
          "Explain field independence and nested object sharing in ReviewDraft. Add a short map of the active feature's source and test targets."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Empty success renders no matches and offline renders retry.",
          "The stored callback does not form an owner cycle, and post-release invocation is safe.",
          "No force unwrap, try!, deliberate crash, live network, or production edit is required.",
          "All outcome cases are covered and the build note distinguishes tools, mode, and platform.",
          "You submit predictions and actual or paper-traced output, plus two concepts to revisit.",
          "Prior lessons and learner progress are not automatically marked complete."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The standalone answer below stores a weak callback because another owner may keep refresh after the screen owner has gone. The fake is a struct with a controlled answer. The owner is a class because this exercise checks reference lifetime. Draft your own version first, then use this program to check your reasoning.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">enum ReviewOutcome: Equatable {\n    case idle\n    case loaded([String])\n    case failed(String)\n}\nenum ReviewFailure: Error { case offline }\nprotocol ReviewServing {\n    func names() throws -&gt; [String]\n}\nstruct ReviewFake: ReviewServing {\n    let result: Result&lt;[String], ReviewFailure&gt;\n    func names() throws -&gt; [String] { try result.get() }\n}\nfinal class ReviewOwner {\n    var state: ReviewOutcome = .idle\n    var refreshCallback: (() -&gt; Void)?\n    let service: any ReviewServing\n\n    init(service: any ReviewServing) {\n        self.service = service\n        refreshCallback = { [weak self] in\n            guard let self else { return }\n            do {\n                self.state = .loaded(try self.service.names())\n            } catch {\n                self.state = .failed(\"Retry\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\nfunc summary(of state: ReviewOutcome) -&gt; String {\n    switch state {\n    case .idle: return \"Start\"\n    case let .loaded(names):\n        return names.isEmpty ? \"No matches\" : \"\\(names.count) providers\"\n    case let .failed(message): return message\n    }\n}\nfunc inspectReviewOwner() {\n    var owner: ReviewOwner? = ReviewOwner(\n        service: ReviewFake(result: .success([]))\n    )\n    let saved = owner?.refreshCallback\n    weak var probe = owner\n    saved?()\n    if let state = owner?.state {\n        print(summary(of: state)) // No matches\n    }\n    owner = nil\n    print(probe == nil) // true\n    saved?()            // missing owner: safely returns\n}\ninspectReviewOwner()\nlet offlineOwner = ReviewOwner(\n    service: ReviewFake(result: .failure(.offline))\n)\nofflineOwner.refreshCallback?()\nprint(summary(of: offlineOwner.state)) // Retry\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The controlled output is No matches, true, and Retry. The saved callback survives but its weak owner becomes nil, so the guard returns. The outcome enum preserves successful empty results and failure as different cases. Catching all errors as Retry is a scoped simplification; a production boundary may preserve typed causes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The initializer stores a newly constructed closure rather than accepting a closure parameter, so there is no parameter to mark escaping. The property's function type does not take an escaping attribute. If a future initializer accepts the stored callback from a caller, that plain closure parameter needs escaping and a fresh ownership review.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Complete your written audit by contrasting this pure program with the active UIKit coordinator. Source and test membership are verified through its Project.swift; the program itself does not verify navigation, task cancellation, localization, signing, or simulator behavior. Choose your next two retrieval tasks from the explanations you had to change, not from the ones that already felt comfortable.\u003c/p>"
      },
