      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Read the active startup paths and design a demo launch experiment. Keep client initialization unchanged and leave measurement fields empty until a run.",
        "steps": [
          "Follow callback spans, window presentation, the loading view, migration, and route readiness from the anchors.",
          "Choose five operations. For each, state its earliest safe phase and required predecessors.",
          "Define signed-out, returning-account, and deep-link fixtures, plus terminated-process and resume preparation.",
          "Keep separate fields for first frame, usable content, first interaction, and exported-size evidence."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The audit distinguishes callback completion from completion of asynchronous startup.",
          "Migration and account reconciliation remain dependencies of consumers that need them.",
          "Cold/warm labels have an explicit preparation procedure and uncertain OS cache state is acknowledged.",
          "No timing, size saving, scene migration, or release-policy claim is fabricated."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Follow required dependencies in the startup source. Restore appearance before creating bars because the delegate says the bars copy the current appearance settings. Reconcile the account before showing an authenticated route. Finish schema migration before a consumer uses the new store format. Leave possibly deferred telemetry undecided until you know its dependencies and what launch evidence it must record. The window callback and loading-view promise have different endings. Returning from the callback does not establish that every later startup job finished.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The experiment sheet records revision, effective configuration, phone/OS, installation history, route fixture, process preparation, cache policy, and thermal condition. Row one is a terminated-process signed-out launch after a previous successful run. Row two is returning from background with process survival verified. Row three is a deep-link launch into a permitted synthetic destination. None is silently renamed “cold.” The default XCTApplicationLaunchMetric result stays in its own SDK-defined launch row. First frame requires a separate supported presentation interval with its actual boundaries; if it is unavailable, that row remains pending. Product readiness has its own state/operation interval, and a follow-up interaction verifies the intended usable-content behavior.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For size, compare the same export/thinning report before and after a hypothetical dependency change, with executable, resources, frameworks, download, and installed figures separated. Leave all observations blank until execution. The recommendation is conditional: “Investigate the longest measured required startup phase; any deferral must preserve account and migration order and must pass first-interaction validation.” That is a concrete, reviewable design even before device access exists.\u003c/p>"
      },
