      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Prepare separate investigations for a typed-expression rewrite and stable deduplication. Check behavior before measuring builds or runtime.",
        "steps": [
          "Define a clean build, a build with no input changes, and an incremental build after one specified file edit. Preserve shared developer caches.",
          "Explain rankedIDs' ordering, temporary arrays, and type inference.",
          "Compare full results from scanningUnique and indexedUnique for empty, repeated, ordered mixed, and long Unicode-string inputs.",
          "Plan several input sizes and memory checks. Name the additional screen-level evidence needed for a user-impact claim."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Both uniqueness functions produce identical full ordered output for the declared fixtures.",
          "Build and runtime performance are reported in separate sections with effective configuration and tool versions.",
          "Input construction is excluded only from a clearly named mapping-only measurement.",
          "Expected complexity and theoretical memory tradeoffs are not reported as observed improvements."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Both functions must turn \u003ccode>[\"b\", \"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\u003c/code> into \u003ccode>[\"b\", \"a\", \"c\"]\u003c/code>. Compare the complete arrays, including order. Also try no items, all equal IDs, all distinct IDs, and mixed Unicode strings under the same Swift String equality rule. For rankedIDs, confirm that ties use ID order and nonpositive scores are omitted. Keep correctness assertions outside the timed block for both candidates. Timing only one version's assertions would make the comparison answer a different question.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The build sheet has independent no-op, private implementation edit, and public contract edit scenarios. Retain the same toolchain, host, destination, resolved dependencies, and declared output/cache preparation. Read timing summaries and the build timeline to identify type checking, dependency emission, linking, resources, or scripts. A source annotation change is a candidate, not a proven improvement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The runtime sheet uses fixed datasets at several sizes, matched release configuration, observable results, and alternating baseline/candidate batches. Record raw times, sample count, median, p95 estimator, range, and allocation or peak evidence. Leave all observations pending until actual execution. The conditional conclusion is: “The membership model predicts less repeated searching with extra storage; device measurements must confirm the mapping tradeoff and a separate screen experiment must establish user impact.” This answer demonstrates contracts and reasoning without pre-completing the learner's profiling work.\u003c/p>"
      },
