      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build the synthetic parser and a pending-route gate in scratch work. Keep local fixture results separate from website association and device-delivery checks.",
        "steps": [
          "Check valid and malformed URLs without opening a network connection.",
          "Store checked LessonRoute values as pending routes instead of raw URL strings.",
          "Use changing tickets for accepted routes and account ownership periods.",
          "Pause fake login, accept a second route, then finish the older login and check that it cannot navigate.",
          "List pending signed-domain, AASA, CDN, device-tap, and App Clip distribution checks."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Only exact allowed hosts and bounded ASCII IDs parse.",
          "Unknown or duplicate queries, encoded slashes, and trailing paths reject.",
          "Only the current route/account generation may navigate after login.",
          "Login cancellation does not navigate or restart a prompt loop.",
          "Parser success is never reported as verified universal-link delivery."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Keep the parser pure and put pending-route decisions in a small MainActor owner. Choose latest-wins explicitly: accepting beta replaces pending alpha. Every new route changes the ticket, even if its value equals the earlier route. Account reset and cancellation invalidate it too. accept returns the ticket to capture before waiting for login. consume checks and clears synchronously. The code below models that one decision. The scene and service must still authorize the actual navigation.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">@MainActor\nfinal class PendingLessonRoute {\n    private var generation = 0\n    private var pending: LessonRoute?\n\n    func accept(_ route: LessonRoute) -&gt; Int {\n        generation += 1\n        pending = route\n        return generation\n    }\n\n    func invalidate() {\n        generation += 1\n        pending = nil\n    }\n\n    func consume(ticket: Int) -&gt; LessonRoute? {\n        guard ticket == generation, let route = pending else {\n            return nil\n        }\n        pending = nil\n        return route\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>\u003ccode>accept\u003c/code> returns the ticket captured before login. \u003ccode>consume\u003c/code> both validates and clears synchronously; repeating it returns nil. Invalidate on account changes and cancellation, then check the scene still owns presentation before navigation. A production implementation also resolves current service authorization, handles feature flags, and defines user feedback when a route cannot be completed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a worked ordering, accept card alpha, pause login, accept card beta, then complete alpha's login. Consuming alpha's ticket returns nil. Complete the current login and consume beta's ticket once. Repeat with account invalidation and A-to-B-to-A; neither should recover alpha's original ticket. Avoid sleeps: explicitly gate fake completion and assert both the count and selected destination.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The parser ledger marks local fixtures as run only after the learner executes them. The association ledger requests the signed app identifier and associated-domains entries, website JSON and HTTPS response without redirects, eligible path rules, current installed build, actual cross-app tap behavior, browser fallback, and App Clip experience configuration. These are planned checks here, not evidence of live company infrastructure.\u003c/p>"
      },
