      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Print valid slot indices and handle missing, empty, and named captions as separate cases.",
        "steps": [
          "Use a half-open range for a nonnegative slot count. Try zero and one too.",
          "Use optional binding for nil, empty text, and Sam. Check emptiness separately.",
          "Write a while loop that prints zero and one and then stops.",
          "Explain on paper why count-minus-one with a closed range can fail, and why an unchanged counter can loop forever.",
          "Read the linked guard/continue example. Say which inputs it skips."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Zero slots print no indices; one slot prints only zero.",
          "The caption outputs distinguish Absent, Blank and the present name.",
          "The while loop terminates through an explicit update.",
          "No invalid range or infinite loop is executed as part of the working exercise."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This answer runs as one file and needs no arrays or functions. The test inputs are values written directly into the program. Predict the output for \u003ccode>slots\u003c/code> equal to zero and \u003ccode>caption\u003c/code> containing empty text. Then try the same optional block with nil and Sam so each path gets its own check.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">let slots = 0\nfor index in 0..&lt;slots {\n    print(index)\n}\nlet caption: String? = \"\"\nif let value = caption {\n    print(value.isEmpty ? \"Blank\" : value)\n} else {\n    print(\"Absent\")\n}\nvar attempt = 0\nwhile attempt &lt; 2 {\n    print(\"Attempt \\(attempt)\")\n    attempt += 1\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>There are no index lines, followed by Blank, Attempt 0 and Attempt 1. The compound assignment \u003ccode>+= 1\u003c/code> is shorthand for reading the integer, adding one and assigning it back. It occurs before the next condition check and is not skipped by any branch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Changing \u003ccode>slots\u003c/code> to one yields one index line, zero. Changing \u003ccode>caption\u003c/code> to nil activates the outer else; changing it to Sam activates the nonempty selection.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A closed zero-through-minus-one range is invalid instead of being a clever way to skip the loop. An unchanged \u003ccode>attempt\u003c/code> would never make the condition false. Explain those outcomes on paper rather than running a hang or trap.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the repository guard, absence of a grouping key triggers continue and excludes that element, which is a business/utility policy in addition to a syntax choice. Record your changed-input evidence; reading this trace does not complete your own practice.\u003c/p>"
      },
