      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Write a value counter with a mutating method and an inout edit. Explain the separate shared-object case.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare Counter with a writable value and mutating increment().",
          "Copy a let original into var edited. Increment only edited.",
          "Write add(_ amount: Int, to value: inout Int). Call it with &edited.value.",
          "Explain on paper why one variable cannot supply two overlapping inout arguments.",
          "Run the Note/Packet example separately. Identify independent fields and the shared Note identity."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Original remains two while edited becomes six after increment and adding three.",
          "Mutation is explicit at the method and inout call sites.",
          "No overlapping inout operation or unsafe pointer is executed.",
          "The explanation labels value fields and reference identity separately."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This counter answer runs by itself. It needs no declaration from the earlier sections. \u003ccode>original\u003c/code> starts at two and stays fixed; \u003ccode>edited\u003c/code> permits changes. Predict the result after one increment and an inout addition of three. The final array edit uses one swapAt operation instead of overlapping inout accesses.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">struct Counter {\n    var value: Int\n    mutating func increment() { value += 1 }\n}\nfunc add(_ amount: Int, to value: inout Int) {\n    value += amount\n}\nlet original = Counter(value: 2)\nvar edited = original\nedited.increment()\nadd(3, to: &amp;edited.value)\nprint(original.value)\nprint(edited.value)\nvar positions = [1, 2]\npositions.swapAt(0, 1)\nprint(positions)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Output is two, six and [2, 1]. The method modifies only \u003ccode>edited\u003c/code>. The inout call temporarily mutates its writable property and ends before the subsequent print. \u003ccode>swapAt\u003c/code> expresses one container operation without two overlapping inout subscript accesses. Explain the invalid same-variable call in notes rather than inserting it into the working program.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For changed-input retrieval, start at four, increment twice and add one. Predict the original and edited values before running. Then contrast this counter with \u003ccode>Packet\u003c/code> containing \u003ccode>Note\u003c/code>: the shared class state requires a separate policy even though the wrapper is a value.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The result is evidence of small language behavior, not a completed client mutation, concurrency proof, or automatic mastery of value ownership.\u003c/p>"
      },
