      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Build a caption base class and a tagged subclass. Check method behavior and downcasts through base references.",
        "steps": [
          "Create Record with required init(name:) and caption().",
          "Create TaggedRecord with tag, a full designated initializer, and a required convenience initializer taking only name.",
          "Override caption and use super.caption() when making its result.",
          "Put a base object and a tagged object in [Record]. Print captions and read tags only after a successful conditional cast.",
          "Explain identity and initialization order. Use no forced casts."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Base caption is Base and tagged caption is Ada [7].",
          "The required convenience path creates tag zero.",
          "Conditional casting fails safely for the base and succeeds for the subclass.",
          "No UIKit imports, forced casts or partially initialized self escape are required."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This complete answer repeats the declarations so you can run it separately. It tests both construction paths and calls through the base type, rather than checking only the concrete subclass. Predict which iteration prints a tag and whether a successful cast keeps the original identity.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">class Record {\n    let name: String\n    required init(name: String) { self.name = name }\n    func caption() -&gt; String { name }\n}\nfinal class TaggedRecord: Record {\n    let tag: Int\n    init(name: String, tag: Int) {\n        self.tag = tag\n        super.init(name: name)\n    }\n    required convenience init(name: String) {\n        self.init(name: name, tag: 0)\n    }\n    override func caption() -&gt; String { \"\\(super.caption()) [\\(tag)]\" }\n}\nlet base = Record(name: \"Base\")\nlet tagged = TaggedRecord(name: \"Ada\", tag: 7)\nlet records: [Record] = [base, tagged]\nfor record in records {\n    print(record.caption())\n    if let special = record as? TaggedRecord { print(special.tag) }\n}\nprint(TaggedRecord(name: \"Default\").tag)\nprint((records[1] as? TaggedRecord) === tagged)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Output is Base, Ada [7], seven, zero and true. The first iteration has no tag line, because the cast returns nil. The last check confirms that the successful cast still refers to \u003ccode>tagged\u003c/code>’s original instance. The base-typed call uses the override, proving that changing the static view did not change the dynamic object.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For retrieval, add another subclass only if its behavior needs a real inherited contract, and predict which calls need \u003ccode>override\u003c/code> versus which construction shapes need \u003ccode>required\u003c/code>. State the separate access change needed for external subclassing: relevant declarations would need open rather than merely public. That statement is a language rule, not a two-module build result.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Independent changed-input practice and recorded evidence remain pending for the learner.\u003c/p>"
      },
