        "solution": "\u003cp>Store the producer's optional Result behind one lock, and keep a separate event log. For success, store .success(17). For failure, store .failure(.fixture). Cancellation before the body starts leaves nil. Give the consumer three branches: apply 17, record the chosen recovery for the error, or record that its prerequisite stopped without output. Capture an immutable scenario value in the producer so later enqueues cannot change which case it represents.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Add consumer.addDependency(producer) before enqueueing either operation. The consumer must then wait until the producer reaches finished. Submitting the consumer first makes the fixture check this rule instead of accidentally relying on arrival order. Have the consumer signal completion to the host, with a finite wait, and inspect the event log afterward. That signal reports completion; the stored Result carries the output. If you also cancel the consumer, do not expect its body to record a recovery event. Check its finished state and lack of side effects instead.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The shipped baseline checks failure recovery and a separate BlockOperation cancelled before start. Build the three-outcome fixture yourself. For a dispatch source, wait for its cancellation handler before reclaiming a descriptor. For a group timeout, report unfinished work rather than assuming it was stopped. Keep QoS as an importance hint. The main-thread-to-worker-to-main drawing has a wait cycle. An asynchronous reply removes the blocking dependency; you can explain that repair without hanging a process.\u003c/p>"
