        "solution": "\u003cp>Start by counting storage and live values separately. scopedSum reserves two uninitialized slots, initializes each to 4, registers defer, and reads the sum 8. Cleanup ends exactly two value lifetimes, then uses deallocate because Swift's typed allocator supplied the storage. In a throwing version, an error after cleanup registration follows that same path. If initialization succeeds for only some elements, track that initialized count and destroy only those elements; blindly deinitializing the entire capacity would be invalid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Create OwnedInt(42). Use borrowing read once or several times, then consuming extract once. The returned Int can outlive the destroyed allocation because it contains the value, not an address. Diagnostics/Ownership.swift compiles the valid path and rejects reuse in its INVALID branch. This verifies the exposed use boundary and the sample result, not every possible unsafe implementation. Exclusivity.swift separately rejects two simultaneous inout accesses to one local variable.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Balance one passRetained with one takeRetainedValue in the synchronous context round trip. The weak observation checks whether that path leaked an extra retain. For repeated callbacks, keep one retain owned by the registration and borrow it in each callback. Stop registration, obtain a guarantee that no callback can still access the context, then release once. A Swift task's cancellation request does not supply that guarantee unless the foreign API documents it. Native callback adapters, platform status paths and sanitizer checks still need separate work. Use this answer as a reference while making your own predictions and collecting evidence.\u003c/p>"
