      "exercise": {
        "brief": "In a personal isolated copy, add a select-two deletion action and test stale selection. Keep draft cancellation separate from accepted task edits.",
        "steps": [
          "Predict A, B, C, D after moving B and D to the front, then after moving B to the end.",
          "Delete a set containing one existing ID and one missing ID; verify the chosen all-or-nothing policy.",
          "Open an editor draft, change text and cancel; design a native observation for unchanged model and file.",
          "Inject a save failure and explain why a valid edit is dirty even if its command returns true."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Moves preserve IDs and selected relative order, including a destination at the end.",
          "A deletion request with an unknown ID changes no accepted tasks.",
          "Cancel leaves the original title and persisted snapshot unchanged.",
          "A save error stays visible and retry persists the latest accepted values."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Begin with deterministic IDs A through D and distinct titles. After moving B and D to boundary zero, assert IDs B, D, A, C. Move B from offset zero to boundary four and expect D, A, C, B. These expectations test the original-array boundary adjustment; simply counting four elements would not detect a wrong move.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For select-two deletion, keep selected IDs in local UI state rather than selected indices. Submit a set containing A and missing X to the companion model. The subset guard fails, so every task remains. Submit A and C next; only D and B remain, in that order. Document that this strict policy rejects stale batches rather than silently removing the remaining selected tasks.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the editor, retain the original file bytes and title before typing a changed draft. Cancel dismisses without calling rename. A native check should verify the title and file remain unchanged after dismissal, and that reopening starts from the accepted value. Host code alone cannot prove dismissal, keyboard focus or retained sheet state, so mark that observation pending until run.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Finally set failSave on the injected store, rename B, and inspect both values: B has the new title in model.items, while isDirty is true and storageError is present. load must refuse to overwrite those unsaved values. Clear the injected fault and call save; the stored snapshot must contain the latest title, and dirty clears only after success. Record the independent batch case and native limits with the command output.\u003c/p>"
      },
