        "solution": "\u003cp>Keep selectedID in one owner and pass its binding to Map. Add demo:ink to the checked catalog, then create both surfaces from that catalog rather than maintaining separate marker and list arrays. Pressing the Ink button sets selectedID to demo:ink and assigns a region centred on its coordinate. The marker tag uses the identical ID. After panning, camera reflects a new viewpoint while selection still identifies Ink until explicitly changed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Capture the final camera update's region at .onEnd. Leave the request-generation code untouched until the person presses Search Apple map data. That makes the testable prediction simple: ten camera updates produce zero search requests, and one search button press creates one request. The visible region is a search hint, not a hard filter of Apple's results.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a saved-catalog rename, preserve selection when demo:ink still exists. For replacement Apple results with response-scoped IDs, clear selection before publishing the new list. Show a normal empty-result message when the new list has no rows. Hiding Map should leave the list and selected ID intact because the model owner sits outside that conditional branch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compile or typecheck the assigned native files against the recorded SDK, then perform interaction checks only if an app is actually built and launched. Record large-text wrapping, selected text value, focus sequence, and failure recovery as observations. The worked answer is a design reference; the learner still supplies the third destination, changed behavior, and evidence. Neither source reading nor a host executable proves that VoiceOver can select the marker on an actual device.\u003c/p>"
