      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Design a recoverable media draft without uploading anything here.",
        "steps": [
          "Choose a stable media ID and predict valid/invalid paths.",
          "Schedule upload success followed by manifest failure, then abandonment.",
          "Add a host assertion for stale upload publication after signout.",
          "Specify manifest rules and a valid-image native check as separately pending integration.",
          "Test abandonment before upload and a dangling published reference; retain the upload generation in cleanup intent."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The orphan has a stable ID and an explicit cleanup owner.",
          "One-mebibyte and one-byte-over boundaries are exact.",
          "A late callback cannot update another account's screen.",
          "Rules declarations are distinguished from actual image validation and rendering.",
          "Abandonment before upload never restarts upload, dangling publication never reports completion, and cleanup cannot delete a recreated path's replacement generation."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Let Alice prepare p1 and store its path as users/alice/media/p1.jpg. An upload success moves the draft to uploadedUnpublished. Manifest failure preserves p1 and the prepared draft; it does not create p2 merely to retry metadata. If Alice abandons the draft, recovery selects removeOrphan and a durable job should own that effect.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Retain a session/request stamp for the upload and compare it before updating either progress or completion. Clearing the old display and calling cancel reduces work; the publication check handles a completion already queued. Add a controlled old-success and old-error schedule rather than relying on a sleep.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use real rules fixtures for the byte boundaries and metadata cases. Design the new manifest schema with owner, media identity, note identity and state, then authorize direct malformed attempts. Since the current rules deny manifest paths, a successful manifest write cannot be claimed from the existing notebook.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Native verification later uses a valid original synthetic JPEG, confirms preparation, upload, bounded download and rendering, and records account-change behavior. The supplied synthetic rules bytes and the offline recovery helper do not prove any of those native behaviors.\u003c/p>\u003cp>With uploaded false, published false and abandoned true, discard the local draft instead of transferring it. With uploaded false and published true, remove the dangling manifest under its reviewed rights. For p1 cleanup, capture generation g1 from the original upload receipt. If a new generation g2 later occupies p1's path, the old command still targets g1 and must preserve g2. Keep generations as decimal strings, without Number conversion.\u003c/p>"
      },
