        "solution": "\u003cp>Use one column at 599. Use two at 600 with ordinary text. Use one at 800 with accessibility text. Add a long synthetic title without changing its ID, so the route still resolves the same notebook. Keep the tile's vertical size driven by content. The expanded label and independent pin button are a better correctness target than equal-height cards that clip text.\u003c/p>\u003cp>In the scratch button, apply padding before the background and observe that the filled region includes the padding. Reverse those modifiers and observe outer padding around the smaller background. Describe the wrapper relationship rather than reporting an invented exact text width. Include the font, text size, and device/window when recording actual bounds.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inspect separate open and pin accessibility controls. Activate pin and verify its value becomes Pinned; activate open and verify the correct ID's detail appears. Check a named accessibility pin action if the card exposes it. Keep navigation distinct from changing state. If one merged element hides either action, revise the semantic grouping.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Host tests remain the oracle for pure column decisions. Native interaction is the oracle for rendered clipping, accessibility exposure, and input behavior. Record results or pending status for narrow/wide, large text, direction, and appearance. A complete learner submission contains the fixture change, predictions, observations, and one remaining limitation rather than a claim that all UI environments were tested.\u003c/p>"
