      "exercise": {
        "brief": "In a scratch copy, predict and implement a Browse refresh with a late old failure. Then justify the smallest boundary needed for a Saved screen.",
        "steps": [
          "Create TinyBrowse or inspect BrowseModel in the companion; list each visible state.",
          "Start request A and hold its result. Start B and return Garden plan. Release A with an offline failure.",
          "Add reset between a third request and its completion. Explain why reset changes revision.",
          "Sketch one shared repository for Browse and Saved with account lifetime, leaving the SwiftUI views as adapters.",
          "Write a costed paragraph comparing a reducer and a coordinator for this exact scope."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "New ready state survives the old ordinary failure.",
          "Reset leaves idle after an obsolete completion.",
          "Task ownership and model retention are named separately.",
          "The repository proposal identifies a shared policy rather than merely adding layers.",
          "No host result is presented as a native interaction check."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The worked schedule starts at revision zero and idle. A starts: revision becomes one, its ticket is one, and state becomes loading. B starts during A's suspension: revision becomes two and B's ticket is two. B returns Garden plan, passes cancellation and revision checks, and assigns ready with that one value. A then throws offline. Its catch sees one does not equal two and returns without assignment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Now C starts with ticket three. Reset raises revision to four and assigns idle. Whether C succeeds or fails, its saved ticket cannot authorize a state change. Canceling C is still useful to conserve work, but this result does not depend on its reader honoring cancellation. A deterministic gate should record that both requests actually started before releasing either result; elapsed sleeps are weak evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For Saved, introduce NoteAccess with notes, saved, toggleSaved, detail, and invalidate operations. An account-scoped repository holds cache and membership. Browse and Saved receive that same owner at composition. Their observable states remain separate. This preserves one source of data policy while allowing independent loading and error presentation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The costed decision keeps MVVM because one refresh and one reset are easy to audit. A reducer would add explicit action and effect wiring, earning its cost if combinations become hard to reproduce. A coordinator would add journey retention and finish rules, earning its cost if the flow crosses presentation systems or must return a parent result. The learner must submit their own changed fixture, predictions, and evidence; reading this answer records no completion.\u003c/p>"
      },
