      "exercise": {
        "brief": "In a scratch copy, add a third feature proposal and trace navigation across A→B→A. Keep the runnable scope synthetic.",
        "steps": [
          "Draw each target arrow using Package.swift; explain which arrow would violate the intended dependency direction.",
          "Construct an n1 route in session A1, switch to B1, and then create A2. Submit the three session identifiers as symbolic names rather than sensitive data.",
          "Try resolving and toggling the A1 route in A2; predict rejection before running.",
          "Propose an Export feature that consumes NoteDomain snapshots without importing BrowseFeature.",
          "Compare one shared path, one path per tab, and a coordinator using owner count and recovery obligations."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The old route fails after A→B→A, despite repeated account text.",
          "An old route cannot mutate new-session saved membership.",
          "The proposed module graph remains acyclic.",
          "The review identifies API, configuration, and lifetime costs.",
          "Scene-local data is not presented as app-wide account synchronization."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The package arrows are BrowseFeature to NoteDomain, SavedFeature to NoteDomain, NoteData to NoteDomain, and AppShell to all four. NoteDomain importing BrowseFeature would reverse the intended contract direction and create a cycle with BrowseFeature's existing dependency. An ExportFeature depending on NoteDomain can receive snapshot values or a narrow read protocol without coupling to Browse's observable state.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A1 begins with a UUID we label S1, account demo-a, and a route detail(S1, demo-a, n1). Switching to B creates S2 and clears path and membership. Returning to A creates S3. The old route still carries S1, so resolve rejects it before repository lookup. Toggle saved uses the same session comparison and returns without changing the new SavedModel. Reusing n1 across fixture accounts makes this test stronger than relying on globally different identifiers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The companion's vertical slice test checks both the initial switch and the return to A. It proves local lifetime rejection, not server authorization. Restoring a record in A2 would require building a new route with current session S3 after validating current access; simply rewriting the UUID on arbitrary old input would bypass the intended restoration policy.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For navigation, retain one shared path when returning to the same root is acceptable. Choose per-tab paths if history preservation is a requirement and budget clearing and restoration for each. Choose a coordinator for a journey with parent completion or mixed presentation systems, and document retention plus every terminal result. The worked choice keeps one Shell path; no client module or Xcode app target is changed by this exercise.\u003c/p>"
      },
