        "prompt": "What does badge() return through FeaturedBadge and through any BadgeNaming? If both callers need the featured behavior, how should the protocol change?",
        "scenario": "BadgeNaming declares no required methods. Its extension adds badge(), returning \"default\". FeaturedBadge adopts BadgeNaming and adds badge(), returning \"featured\". Call the method first on FeaturedBadge, then on any BadgeNaming containing that same value.",
        "androidBridge": "Kotlin extension functions are statically selected and provide a useful analogy for Swift extension-only helpers. A Swift protocol requirement with a default implementation participates in protocol dispatch; a Kotlin extension cannot retroactively add interface conformance.",
        "rubric": [
          "Predicts featured for the concrete call and default for the protocol-valued call.",
          "Distinguishes an extension helper from a method the protocol requires.",
          "Adds badge() to the protocol when callers need each type's own implementation.",
          "Explains that any hides the concrete type and can contain a struct as well as a class."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The concrete FeaturedBadge call returns featured. The \u003ccode>any BadgeNaming\u003c/code> call returns default. Here \u003ccode>any\u003c/code> means the caller uses a value through its protocol without knowing the concrete type. The protocol never promised a badge method. Its extension only supplied a helper, so the same-named method on FeaturedBadge does not replace that helper for calls through this protocol.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If callers require specialized behavior through the protocol, declare \u003ccode>func badge() -&gt; String\u003c/code> inside \u003ccode>BadgeNaming\u003c/code>. The extension can still provide the default implementation, and the conformer supplies its own implementation for the requirement. An existential is a value used through a protocol with its concrete type hidden. Structs as well as classes can meet that protocol. Test the same behavior through the boundary consumers actually use. Testing only the concrete type can miss this discrepancy. Extensions can add methods and computed properties, but cannot introduce stored instance properties to keep hidden badge state.\u003c/p>",
