        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The navigation stack still owns the counter controller while detail is shown. Returning reveals that same controller, whose stored count is two. It need not build its views again. Put one-time view construction in the loading path. Decide separately whether each return should refresh data. Visibility tells you what is shown; ownership tells you what stays alive.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Controller initialization, view loading, appearance, layout, scene activity, and deallocation describe different events. Accessing the view can load it without making it visible. Layout and appearance callbacks can repeat. Disappearance can mean coverage, a transition, or removal, so it does not by itself justify reporting cancellation. Inspect the navigation or presentation event that ends the flow. A popped controller may still have another strong owner, and a retaining task may delay deallocation. Use deinit as lifetime evidence after auditing owners, while performing timely cleanup at the explicit stopping event required by the feature.\u003c/p>",
