        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The task timeout chooses a result but cannot force the other child to finish. A transport timeout is a separate mechanism. A task group waits for its children when its scope ends, including canceled children. If a callback source keeps waiting, the group can remain open after the deadline. A checked continuation bridges one callback result into an async wait. It does not automatically stop the callback source when cancellation is requested.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Define how the bridge cancels its underlying operation and how the continuation completes when cancellation races a normal callback. A checked continuation must resume exactly once; a cancellation handler may run concurrently, so protect the completion state with a suitable synchronized mechanism rather than an unsafely shared Boolean. If the source cannot be stopped, the design must state that limitation and assign an owner to any work that continues independently. Test completion-before-cancellation and cancellation-before-completion using gates, then join every task. Do not recover by swallowing cancellation into an empty success or by promising a hard deadline that the dependency cannot honor.\u003c/p>",
