        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>\u003ccode>bufferingNewest(1)\u003c/code> keeps at most one waiting value. A newer progress update can replace an older buffered update. That works when each value fully describes current progress and skipped intermediate states are allowed. It does not make the producer wait: continuation yield reports whether a value was enqueued, dropped, or the stream ended. Limiting a buffer and applying producer backpressure are different promises.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Ordered text fragments may depend on every earlier fragment, so dropping them can corrupt the output. Use complete cumulative snapshots or a producer-consumer protocol that explicitly provides backpressure and required-event handling. A one-element bound also does not bound bytes if an element can be enormous. Define production start time, consumer count, overflow, failure, and stopping behavior. Constructing a stream can start its producer before iteration, and one AsyncStream is not automatically a broadcast channel. Observe yield results when loss matters and provide explicit early-stop behavior rather than assuming a loop break cancels every retained producer.\u003c/p>",
