        "level": "Staff",
        "kind": "Design",
        "minutes": 16,
        "prompt": "Own a threat model for a linked reminder. Compare controls for specific attacker actions, then define verification, rollout gates, recovery, and remaining risks.",
        "scenario": "A reminder parses an external link, reads a token, displays account-scoped cached metadata, and sends diagnostics. The team suggests trusting the link host, logging complete errors, and using pinning as a universal fix.",
        "androidBridge": "Android app links, Keystore, TLS, and privacy reviews suggest trust-boundary questions. Universal-link association does not authorize parameters, Keychain is not a complete Keystore equivalent, and ATS, bearer authentication, and server authorization remain different iOS defenses.",
        "rubric": [
          "Maps assets and link, storage, service, and telemetry boundaries with owners.",
          "Compares validation, minimization, Keychain policy, and pinning against concrete threats.",
          "Defines synthetic tests and bounded rollout gates, including account changes.",
          "States recovery limits and distinguishes manifests from runtime or compliance proof."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A threat model names valuable data, possible attackers, and where protections apply. Give the feature owner route parsing and account checks, the service owner authorization, and the platform owner credential policy. Link parameters remain untrusted even for an associated domain. Compare strict parsing, account-cache cleanup, and allowlisted diagnostics first. TLS and ATS protect transport; they do not prove this user may access a particular appointment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pinning restricts accepted server certificates or keys. Evaluate it against a specific attacker and name rotation and recovery owners. A broken pin can fail before remote configuration is reachable. Verify denied routes, wrong-account cache access, locked-item failures, and diagnostic captures using synthetic data. Gate deployment on those controls and artifact review, then observe bounded operational outcomes. Recovery may narrow the affected feature, restore compatible service behavior, or require a corrective binary; it cannot assume every offline client honors a new flag. Privacy manifests describe applicable behavior, but packaging intent alone proves neither runtime practice nor compliance. Record residual risk and specialist-review needs.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "Why doesn't hashing an identifier guarantee anonymity?",
          "What changes if trust evaluation fails before configuration can load?"
        ],
        "pitfalls": [
          "Treating universal links or TLS as authorization to access a resource.",
          "Calling pinning or one privacy manifest a complete security guarantee."
        ]
