        "prompt": "Which view should take extra width, and which should give up width? Explain the separate jobs of hugging and compression resistance.",
        "scenario": "The title prefers 180 points, the badge 60, and spacing uses 12. The parent gives the row either 300 or 220 points. Choose horizontal priorities for the desired behavior.",
        "minutes": 5,
        "androidBridge": "ConstraintLayout and Compose measurement help frame size negotiation, but Swift intrinsic-size priorities are optional inequalities, not Kotlin layout weights or Compose hard constraints. Raising hugging does not raise compression resistance.",
        "rubric": [
          "Explains hugging as resistance to growth and compression resistance as resistance to shrinking.",
          "Calculates 48 extra points and a 32-point shortage.",
          "Chooses separate horizontal priorities from the intended behavior.",
          "Explains how a known narrower width can make multiline text taller.",
          "Identifies required-rule conflicts and a fallback when minimum readable widths cannot fit."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Intrinsic content size is a view's preferred size. Hugging resists growing beyond it; compression resistance resists becoming smaller. The row prefers \u003ccode>180 + 60 + 12 = 252\u003c/code> points. At 300 it has 48 extra points: lower title hugging lets the title grow. At 220 it lacks 32 points: lower title compression resistance makes the title narrow first. These are separate preferences.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A multiline title can convert narrower width into additional height, provided its width is determined and vertical constraints allow growth. The arithmetic predicts negotiation, not real font measurements. Inspect the actual instance priorities rather than assuming every control uses identical defaults. Required priority is 1000. Incompatible rules all marked required cannot be satisfied together. If the badge and minimum readable title cannot fit, change to a vertical arrangement or another declared overflow policy. Verify narrow width, long localized text, and accessibility text sizes before accepting the row.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "What if title width is undetermined or it allows only one line?",
          "How would you verify which view gives way without equal competing priorities?"
        ],
