        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A cursor identifies the next page; a query generation identifies the current search. The page owner keeps both, the active cursor, ordered IDs, and whether paging ended. Start only if that generation and cursor have no active request. Capture them before waiting and check them on success and failure before changing rows, errors, or active markers. An old completion must not unlock newer work. Serialize loads or explicitly buffer out-of-order pages.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Preserve useful rows on a later-page failure and retry the failed cursor under the same guard. Define duplicate-ID merging and repeated-cursor behavior. Prefetch is a separate resource-interest mechanism: translate callback positions to current IDs, and ensure visible configuration can load missing content without any prefetch callback. Canceling one speculative interest should not cancel a shared image still needed by a visible consumer. Use controlled start signals and completion gates to release B before A, then exercise A's success and error paths.\u003c/p>",
