        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>An optional String either contains text or is absent, called nil. Empty text is like a blank card; nil means there is no card. The empty String is still present, so \u003ccode>blank ?? \"Unknown\"\u003c/code> returns empty content. Nil selects Unknown and Ada remains Ada. If the product rejects empty or whitespace-only text, add a separate content policy instead of treating coalescing as validation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Int8(exactly: 127)\u003c/code> succeeds because the value is representable in signed eight-bit storage; 128 yields nil. Nil tells you the number does not fit. It avoids forcing a conversion that cannot preserve the value. Handle it through binding or an explicit domain failure rather than force unwrapping. A display fallback should not overwrite a missing identifier with zero, because later code may mistake the sentinel for real data. These test inputs check absence and the limits of the numeric type; they do not verify localization or every numeric input. Keep the rejected input visible when a user needs a reason.\u003c/p>",
