        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A designated initializer is a main construction path. In a subclass, it must initialize the stored properties that subclass adds before calling the immediate superclass’s designated initializer. Assign tag first, then call super.init for inherited state. A convenience initializer starts by calling another initializer in the same class. That chain must eventually reach a designated initializer. Required obliges subclasses to provide its construction shape through permitted inheritance or implementation; implementing an already required initializer uses required rather than adding override.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A plain Record is a valid element in this Array. As! assumes it is a TaggedRecord and traps when that assumption is false. Use as? with optional binding, or redesign the consumer around a polymorphic requirement if tag-specific behavior is not necessary. A successful cast supplies a narrower view of the original object; it does not clone it. Test base and tagged instances plus the default convenience path. Unsupported archival construction that traps in a UIKit initializer is a separate programmer restriction, not ordinary failable input validation.\u003c/p>",
