        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The action may be absent. If present, it is a function you can call. \u003ccode>(() -&gt; Void)?\u003c/code> is an optional function value accepting no inputs and returning no useful result. Action?() invokes it only if that function value is present. Install keeps the callback for later, like saving a phone number rather than using it only during this call. Its plain callback parameter therefore needs @escaping. Put that attribute on the parameter declaration. A closure constructed internally for a property has no accepted plain parameter to annotate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Escaping means the function value may live beyond the accepting call. Scheduling, weak capture, cancellation and promised delivery are separate contracts. Tap in this fixture runs synchronously. Clearing the property releases Button’s ownership of the action and makes later local taps no-ops, but a separately saved action may still exist. Review what that action captures and who owns it. Optional closure parameters have different type behavior, so do not mechanically apply the plain-parameter annotation rule to every optional function argument. State delivery and teardown contracts separately when adapting a real API.\u003c/p>",
