        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Serial means destination items run one at a time. Concurrent permits overlap. Sync makes the submitting caller wait; async lets it continue before completion. These are two separate choices. A serial async write returns to its caller sooner while the queue still runs one item at a time. A concurrent sync submission can overlap other destination items but still blocks its caller until that item finishes. Neither creates a guaranteed dedicated thread; the main queue is the important main-thread exception.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With an async setter, the caller can return before the cache changes. Check what later reads and completion callbacks are promised to observe. A synchronous read on the same owned serial queue can observe an earlier submitted write, provided the caller is outside the queue and the wait graph has no cycle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Calling sync from the queue's own running item prevents the new item from starting until its waiter returns. A main thread waiting for a worker that in turn waits for the main thread forms an indirect cycle. Use asynchronous completion where waiting is unnecessary. QoS indicates workload importance, not a deadline or order across unrelated queues; it cannot repair these dependency errors.\u003c/p>",
