        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>State labels the login attempt so its callback can be matched. PKCE, Proof Key for Code Exchange, separately requires the code exchange to prove knowledge of the verifier behind the authorization challenge. I would validate the exact callback destination and reject missing, wrong or duplicate state and code values. A correct-looking URL is not sufficient.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Accept the matching callback once. Then exchange its code using that attempt’s verifier. The mock provider checks S256 and consumes the code once. The fixed verifier demonstrates encoding against a reference vector; it is not production entropy or a complete provider implementation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The exchange may suspend. A newer login or logout can take ownership while the old exchange still succeeds. Success alone does not grant permission to install its credentials. Credential installation must check the captured epoch at the final owner and save the vault before publishing a durable session. A storage failure stays distinct from denial or user cancellation. I would order a late-result test explicitly. Browser presentation, random generation, redirect registration, identity verification and server authorization need separate platform/provider evidence.\u003c/p>",
