        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Check the epoch where account reset and the actual fetch, change and save are ordered together. An epoch is a session generation; account A returning later does not make an old A generation current. Checking on an actor and then awaiting another queue leaves a window in which logout can win. A queued closure must validate the captured account and epoch before touching managed state.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The lab gives a dedicated main-queue writer a MainActor owner. That specific setup establishes its bridge; it is not an assumption valid for any context. It resets and rotates epoch during activation, enters performAndWait for mutation, and asserts the established MainActor bridge inside the Sendable closure. No await separates the guard from save. A private context would need its own serialized command owner, not the same assumption.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Transfer prepared values as Sendable snapshots. Sendable concerns safe transfer of these values; awaiting the context operation separately permits suspension. Managed objects stay inside the context queue, and failed saves roll back the dedicated writer's whole pending scope. That design would be wrong if unrelated UI edits shared the context. I would test A-to-B-to-A with an old epoch and read through a fresh context after a valid commit, keeping in-memory host evidence separate from disk restart and device protection.\u003c/p>",
