        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Start from the original fixture for each change. That way each result tests one rule rather than several accumulated changes. A timeout with null correctness and duration remains an attempted outcome and increments timeout count. It is excluded from success latency. Nineteen successes remain, below the lab’s minimum, so the result is insufficient_samples. It is not replaced with zero or removed from the raw array.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A fast wrong answer is still wrong. An incorrect outcome with correct false yields correctness_failure. A success with no elapsed duration yields invalid_capture because a successful latency record must contain a finite nonnegative duration and assert correct true. The validator retains cohort summaries and issues without granting release approval. The fictional twenty-value baseline from 80 through 99 has median 89.5. For nearest-rank p95, sort the values and select position ceil(0.95 × count), counting from one. Here ceil means rounding a fractional position up to the next whole number. For 20 values, choose the 19th, giving p95 98. The candidate values from 70 through 89 have median 79.5 and p95 88. Those values test arithmetic only. I would also verify unique identities, one revision per variant and complete observed provenance before comparing real samples, keeping cancellation and preparation counts visible beside failure rate.\u003c/p>",
