        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>List which consumers are eligible and give each a stable ID before counting adoption. Otherwise the denominator can change to hide difficult integrations. An adopted row needs accepted behavior checks, build/target coordinates, relevant resources and interactions, and owner acceptance. Adding a dependency is a leading indicator, not consumer proof. Pending consumers and exceptions remain visible in the denominator.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Name separate responsibilities. The platform owner owns the schema and exception policy. Consumer owners own native behavior. Service and release owners preserve compatibility for installed clients. I fund fixtures, adapter support, integration help, and temporary dual-path maintenance so adoption has practical incentives rather than only a mandate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An exception needs a reason, an owner, a baseline, an extra check to cover its risk and a trigger for review. Source deprecation warnings can start before removal, but compiler guidance does not change installed binaries. Retirement requires supported consumer evidence, compatibility review, and comparable operational outcomes. The local gate checks record completeness and attachment consistency; authentic cross-team acceptance and customer outcomes remain external evidence, never synthetic fixture achievements.\u003c/p>",
