        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Soft reset to the private commit's parent moves HEAD while retaining the index and working tree, leaving Notes.txt staged as an addition. Mixed reset to the current HEAD then clears that index addition while keeping Notes.txt in the working directory; because the parent lacks the path, it becomes untracked. A named rescue branch created beforehand retains the original committed notes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Revert of the bad non-merge policy commit records a new inverse change, restoring 80 while the bad commit remains an ancestor. That distinction matters when others depend on the history. Restore is a selected-path copy operation; hard reset also overwrites tracked working content and can remove obstructing untracked paths. No reset reference preserves all uncommitted bytes automatically. Diagnose location and ownership first, then verify both content and graph. The runner uses only disposable scratch data.\u003c/p>",
