        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Friendly client validation improves the user's experience, but an attacker can skip it. Direct rules fixtures must submit forged ownerID, extra admin keys, wrong types, invalid title sizes, stale revisions and client timestamps. The real rules compare authenticated UID to the path and validate the proposed record. A local Swift function receiving uid = alice cannot prove that evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The host and rule validators are intentionally not identical. Swift trims and counts characters; the rules inspect stored string size without trimming. The host restricts note IDs while the current Firestore rules do not. Explain these differences and choose a deliberate rule-hardening exercise instead of claiming equivalence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Run actual emulator assertions for both allowed and rejected cases once provisioned. Mocked authenticated contexts execute the rules engine, but do not prove provider login. Preserve that evidence distinction in the report. A passing host baseline or an empty screen for Bob is insufficient proof of authorization enforcement.\u003c/p>",
