        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A system picker can grant access to a chosen asset under its platform contract. That does not establish permission to publish it or ownership of a backend resource. The product asks for upload intent, prepares appropriate image bytes and submits them under the current authenticated account. Storage rules then authorize the path and validate permitted metadata.\u003c/p>\u003cp>contentType = image/jpeg and a .jpg filename describe what the bytes claim to be. The supplied three-byte fixture is not a decodable image; it intentionally exercises rules declarations and rights. Native decoding and rendering need a separate valid original synthetic image.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Bound prepared data before upload and constrain decoding memory too. One mebibyte of encoded data can have a different decoded-memory footprint. Restrict paths, use immutable media identities and keep tokens or shared download capabilities out of private manifests unless their rights are reviewed. A host can model these policies but cannot establish actual Storage enforcement.\u003c/p>",
