        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Upload and manifest publication are separate effects. If p1 uploads but publication fails, retain uploadedUnpublished with the same media ID. A retry should retry the manifest rather than invent another object identity. If Alice abandons the draft, choose removeOrphan and assign a cleanup owner.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Persist the intended cleanup in a durable job before claiming recovery will survive restart. Deletion can succeed while job acknowledgement fails, so repeated deletion must retain the original object's generation precondition. It reconciles absence and preserves a replacement at the same path. Keep progress visible and avoid silently forgetting failed cleanup. The host recovery helper only selects a next step; it neither persists nor executes that job.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Cancel an account-owned transfer on departure and compare its captured account/request stamp before publishing progress or completion. Already queued callbacks can outlive cancellation. The current Firestore notebook rules deny manifest paths, so a manifest schema, rules fixtures and integration are additional work. Do not describe these sources as a completed photo application.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Abandonment before upload discards the local draft. A published reference without an upload needs manifest repair, not completion. Carry the upload generation as a decimal string.\u003c/p>",
