        "id": "iq-147-a",
        "lesson": "147",
        "level": "Senior",
        "kind": "Code",
        "minutes": 9,
        "prompt": "Describe the checks and suspension points required before a repository commits a snapshot.",
        "scenario": "An actor loads a complete snapshot, awaits an injected clock, then replaces its cache. Logout or a newer fetch may enter during either await.",
        "androidBridge": "An isolated Swift actor does not behave like holding a Java monitor across a blocking operation. Kotlin Mutex and coroutine cancellation semantics must be chosen explicitly rather than assumed equivalent.",
        "rubric": [
          "Explains actor reentrancy in familiar terms.",
          "Saves an admitted-fetch ticket before reader suspension.",
          "Checks cancellation, account activity, and current generation after all awaits.",
          "Validates duplicate/invalid values before replacement.",
          "Commits and reconciles complete-snapshot membership without another suspension."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>First check cancellation and active account. Read monotonic time, then recheck active authority because clock access can suspend. If an allowed cache is fresh, return it. Otherwise increment generation and save the ticket before awaiting the reader.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After the reader returns, obtain its acceptance time, then check cancellation and active state again. Compare the saved ticket with current generation. Validate all identifiers and titles, rejecting duplicate identifiers before replacing the last good snapshot. Assign cache and intersect saved membership with the complete snapshot without another await between final checks and commit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The actor prevents arbitrary concurrent field access but permits other actor work while this method is suspended. A newer admitted fetch or invalidate can therefore change authority. A failed refresh preserves the last good cache, and a revoked account rejects commits even when the reader ignores cancellation. Disk atomicity and provider authorization are separate promises not established by this in-memory transition.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "Why is absence from a paginated page insufficient to remove a saved identifier?",
          "If B fails, should A's older successful response be accepted under this policy?"
        ],
